// Regression: `select` is a CONTEXTUAL keyword - a statement only when `{` follows.
//
// `select` is channel multiplexing (`select { v = ch.recv() => ... }`), but it is
// also an obvious name for a MODULE: a selection engine, a query builder. The word
// used to be reserved unconditionally, so:
//
//   * `import select` failed with "Expected module name" pointing at the import
//     line, never mentioning that the word was a keyword, and then cascaded into
//     a parse error for every later statement.
//   * `select.clip(c)` failed with "Expected an expression" at the START of the
//     line, and recovery skipped to the next `;` or `}` - so one such line
//     poisoned the rest of the function.
//
// WynCanvas hit both with src/select.wyn and had to ship a `selection.wyn` symlink.
//
// This file keeps BOTH readings working. The `select { }` half is the CONTROL: a
// fix that made the name work by weakening the statement would pass a name-only
// test, and channel multiplexing would break silently.
//
// STILL RESERVED, deliberately: `var select`, a struct FIELD and a function
// PARAMETER named `select`. Those positions expect a type or a binding after the
// name and are a separate, larger change. A module name and a qualified call were
// what blocked real code, and are what this covers.
// EXPECT: 7
// EXPECT: 14
// EXPECT: done
import select

fn main() {
    // A module named `select`, and a qualified call on it. This is the shape
    // WynCanvas needed and could not spell.
    println(select.seven())
    println(select.twice(7))

    // The `select { }` STATEMENT control lives in select_stmt_still_parses.wyn:
    // `Channel` is not resolvable in a file that imports a module, which is a
    // separate defect, so the two halves cannot share one file.

    println("done")
}
